Assistant: Sure!  A loop is a group of instructions that you need to perform repeatedly.  Loops commonly use the "for" and "while" control structures to create a loop.  Below are examples showing each control structure:

For loops: the for loop specifies a variable count, the loop will run until count reaches a specified value.  For example, if we declare a variable, say a, we can use the following code to repeatedly print a to the screen:

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    System.out.println(a);
}

There are three main things to keep in mind about these loops:

You begin by specifying a number (or number of iterations)  that follows this format:  "for [variable]".  The variable is called the "loop variable".

The for loop then checks its condition throughout the loop, and the code inside will be executed as long as the condition is true.  You can write the code inside the loop block in whatever style you want, but you usually use a semicolon to separate the statements.

The loop will eventually end when the value of loop variable is 10.  The statement at the end of the loop will always execute, even if there are remaining iterations.

You can add statements to perform other actions, so for example you could add to the statement to print out the following:

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    System.out.println("I can do math with i");
}

or

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    Math.abs(10 - i) + 1;
}

Finally, here is an example using a while loop to increment a variable:

int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i = i + 1;
}

You see that we need to specify a starting value for the variable and to repeat the action as long as the condition is true. We also need to add a statement to print out the result of the increment, and finally, we will add a statement to add the new value of the variable to its old value and print out the new value of the variable.

